WEBVTT

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:07.634
Taylor polynomials imitate a function near one point by matching its value and successive derivatives there

00:00:07.634 --> 00:00:14.868
For exponential x at zero, every derivative equals one, so the coefficients are reciprocals of factorials

00:00:14.868 --> 00:00:18.033
More matching derivatives improve the local shape.

00:00:19.333 --> 00:00:26.070
At one fifth, the cubic polynomial gives about one point two two one three three three

00:00:26.070 --> 00:00:29.860
This is an approximation, not an exact finite identity

00:00:29.860 --> 00:00:35.333
Taylor's remainder theorem controls the difference using a bound on the next derivative.

00:00:36.633 --> 00:00:43.681
On the interval from zero to one fifth, the fourth derivative is exponential x and is less than two

00:00:43.681 --> 00:00:51.100
Therefore the cubic error is at most two times one fifth to the fourth, divided by twenty-four: about one ten-thousandth.

00:00:52.400 --> 00:00:55.691
A power series also has a convergence domain

00:00:55.691 --> 00:01:03.096
The geometric expansion for one over one minus x works only when absolute x is less than one

00:01:03.096 --> 00:01:09.267
Outside that interval its terms may not shrink, even though the original function remains defined.
